            <h3>High-Speed vs Regional Trains </h3>
            <p>High-Speed (Acela): While not true European-style high-speed rail, the Acela operates at speeds up to 150 mph on certain segments of the Northeast Corridor, making it the fastest option between Baltimore and D.C. The Acela offers a premium experience with leather seating, quiet cars, and complimentary beverages.</p>

            <p>Regional Trains (Northeast Regional & MARC): These services operate at lower speeds with more frequent stops but offer greater affordability and accessibility. The Northeast Regional provides a comfortable intercity experience, while MARC serves as essential commuter infrastructure for the Baltimore-Washington corridor.</p>

            <h3>What This Means for Travelers </h3>
            <ul>
                <li>The Acela shaves significant time off the journey for those willing to pay a premium.</li>
                <li>Northeast Regional trains offer the best balance of speed, comfort, and price for most travelers.</li>
                <li>MARC is unbeatable for budget-conscious daily commuters, despite the extra stops and longer travel time.</li>
                <li>All services deliver you directly to city-center stations, eliminating the need for additional ground transportation.</li>
            </ul>
