            <h2>Baltimore Penn Station (Main Station) </h2>
            <p>Baltimore Penn Station is the primary departure point for all Baltimore-to-Washington, D.C. rail service and one of the busiest stations on the Northeast Corridor. This beautifully restored 1911 Beaux-Arts building is not just a transit hub; it's a historic landmark that welcomes nearly 1 million Amtrak passengers annually.</p>

            <h3>Location & Accessibility: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Situated at 1500 N. Charles Street, just north of downtown Baltimore</li>
                <li>Easily accessible via Light RailLink, local buses, and major roadways</li>
                <li>The Johns Hopkins and Baltimore College town shuttles serve the station, making it convenient for students</li>
                <li>Short ride or walk to downtown Baltimore, Inner Harbor, and Mount Vernon cultural district</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Key Facilities: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Ticketing:</b> Full-service Amtrak ticket counters and self-service kiosks; MARC ticket vending machines</li>
                <li><b>Waiting Areas:</b> Spacious, climate-controlled waiting hall with historic architecture and comfortable seating</li>
                <li><b>Food &amp; Retail: </b>Multiple cafés, coffee shops, and retail vendors for snacks, beverages, and travel essentials</li>
                <li><b>Parking: </b>Multi-level parking garage adjacent to the station (paid parking available)</li>
                <li><b>Restrooms: </b>Clean, well-maintained facilities located on the main concourse</li>
                <li><b>Accessibility:</b> Fully ADA-compliant with elevators, ramps, and accessible restrooms</li>
                <li>Wi-Fi: Free Wi-Fi available throughout the station</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Services: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Amtrak:</b> Northeast Regional, Acela, and long-distance services including Vermonter, Palmetto, Carolinian, Crescent, and Silver Service</li>
                <li><b>MARC: </b>Penn Line commuter rail with frequent weekday service to Washington, D.C.</li>
                <li><b>Light RailLink: </b>Connects Penn Station to downtown Baltimore, BWI Airport, and northern suburbs</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Downtown departures offer the best flexibility, most frequent connections, and easiest access to Baltimore's attractions</li>
                <li>The historic station itself is worth experiencing its grand architecture and high ceilings create an impressive travel environment.</li>
                <li>Arrive 15-20 minutes early for MARC (first-come, first-served seating) or 30 minutes for Amtrak (reserved seating, ticket verification)</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>BWI Airport Rail Station (Limited Connections) </h2>
            <p>BWI Airport Rail Station provides a convenient alternative for travelers coming from or going to BWI Thurgood Marshall Airport. While not a primary departure point for city-to-city travelers starting in downtown Baltimore, it's invaluable for air-rail connections.</p>

            <h3>Location & Accessibility: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Located at 2 Amtrak Way, Linthicum Heights, approximately 10 miles south of downtown Baltimore</li>
                <li>Connected to BWI Airport terminals via free shuttle bus service (runs every 10-15 minutes)</li>
                <li>Accessible via MD-295 (Baltimore-Washington Parkway)</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Key Facilities: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Ticketing: </b>Self-service ticket vending machines for Amtrak and MARC; no full-service counter</li>
                <li><b>Waiting Area: </b>Covered, climate-controlled waiting area with basic seating</li>
                <li><b>Parking:</b> Limited paid parking available; primarily designed for drop-off and pick-up</li>
                <li><b>Restrooms:</b> Basic restroom facilities</li>
                <li><b>Accessibility:</b> ADA-compliant with ramps and accessible pathways to shuttle buses</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Services: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Amtrak: </b>Northeast Regional, Acela, and select long-distance services</li>
                <li><b>MARC: </b>Penn Line commuter rail (limited stops compared to Penn Station)</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers: </h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Airport Connections: </b>Ideal if you're flying into BWI and continuing to Washington, D.C., by train, no need to go into downtown Baltimore</li>
                <li><b>Limited Departures: </b>Not all trains stop here; check schedules carefully, as some Amtrak and MARC services bypass this station</li>
                <li><b>Extra Planning Required: </b>If starting your journey from downtown Baltimore, Penn Station is more convenient, with better facilities and more frequent service</li>
                <li><b>Shuttle Connection:</b> Factor in 10-15 minutes for the airport shuttle when planning your connection time</li>
            </ul>
